Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action; 1990s Milwaukee into later Seattle. Closed cast. NARRATOR is the first-person hero (name withheld until the final beat per source).

FADE IN:

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Rain beads on a high window. City lights smear below. NARRATOR, forties, neat but tired, sits in a plain chair facing us. A cat sleeps on the couch. A half-packed overnight bag waits by the door.

(SOUND: rain on glass, distant ferry horn)

NARRATOR
Big brother was mine. Not the book. My real big brother. Nick.

He almost smiles. It dies.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. Face like a puzzle held with glue-gun scars. Chin. Left eyebrow. When he walked in, people moved.

CUT TO:

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT BUILDING - DAY

A big brick fifth-floor walk-up. Milwaukee. Late afternoon grit.

(SOUND: traffic, distant train)

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Cramped. Game-show noise from a small TV. MA, older, tired brown eyes, afghan on her lap, stares at the screen. Young NARRATOR, twenties, plain work clothes, sets grocery bags down. NICK fills the doorway — tall, reddish-brown mess of hair, scars on chin and over left eyebrow, burnt-orange energy even standing still.

(SOUND: TV applause, fridge hum)

NICK
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

NARRATOR
I don't know. Why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. That's how old people go downhill.

Nick drops cash on the counter, already turning to leave.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He ran a line at the metal factory. Drove a burnt-orange Trans Am with the eagle on the hood. Lived five blocks away and still ran this place.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Weights. Mirrors. A GUY on the curl bench sees Nick coming, drops a forty-pound bar, steps aside. Nick nods, racks fifty like warm-up.

(SOUND: iron plates, grunts)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
People took notice. Man, did they move.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND KIRK STREET - DAY (FLASH, BRIEF)

Kids' kickball. A ball smashes through a kitchen window. LITTLE MARK freezes. Young Nick steps up to furious MRS. ENGEL.

NICK
I did it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He never told Ma it was Mark. None of us did. Somehow a twelve-year-old came up with sixty bucks for the glass.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma lines pill bottles in a careful row. Narrator watches, uneasy.

(SOUND: pills rattling, clock tick)

MA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

NARRATOR
Ma. We don't go to church anymore. Your pills are right in front of you.

Her eyes go glassy, empty — then snap back.

MA
Oh. Right. I don't know what got into me.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

Narrator locks a bike. A girl — SIONNA, skinny, pale, short brown hair, light blue eyes, black V-neck, fish-symbol necklace, backpack — trips on the cement and brushes his shoulder.

(SOUND: street noise, bike chain)

NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes her shirt. They look at each other too long.

NARRATOR
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

NARRATOR
Sionna? That's different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
Irish goddess. River Shannon.

He is not really listening. Those eyes — early-morning blue.

NARRATOR
You want to come in? Pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don't eat pizza. But if you want a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She gives him a number. Walks away. Looks back once over her shoulder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I knew her. Like the tide coming in at the ocean that one time in New Jersey. I didn't want to move my feet.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Packers on the TV. Nick slaps Narrator's back. Pool balls crack.

(SOUND: bar crowd, game crowd roar, cue ball break)

NICK
Hey, little brother. What's shakin'?

NARRATOR
Ma was confused today. Church. Mrs. Canasta. Empty eyes.

NICK
I knew she'd go crazy cooped up. Take her to the store. Do something before we pay for a nursing home. I don't have money for that shit.

Nick's look could punch. Narrator pays and leaves.

EXT. CITY LIBRARY - NIGHT

Narrator pushes in out of the cold. Old wood floors, chain-hung lights, classic literature stacks.

(SOUND: quiet room tone, pages, distant heater)

He fills a temporary card. Checks out WUTHERING HEIGHTS.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NARRATOR'S ROOM - NIGHT

Narrator reads in bed. Ma already asleep.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Heathcliff and Catherine. I mixed Sionna into the dream. Old clothes. Horses. Moors.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College backpacks, steam, chatter. Sionna at a back table — green hooded top, those eyes. Narrator sits, out of place.

(SOUND: espresso machine, low indie music)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. Guy and girl. Grew apart. Still in love.

SIONNA
I know. I read it.

She talks ghosts, doubles, moors — wild, infertile country. Love that never changes destroys you.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something inside me woke up. We made a date for Friday. Walking to the bus I thought of Ma — Dad left and she never moved on. That kind of love can kill you too.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Purple mats. Narrator in T-shirt and shorts, shirt falling over his face in downward dog. Sionna beside him, easy. Twenty bodies breathe together.

(SOUND: instructor's calm voice, collective breath)

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. That's why they call it practice.

After: Narrator lies in savasana, actually calm.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Nick stomps. Ma flinches in her chair.

(SOUND: door slam echo, floorboards)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment, you ass. Where the hell were you?

NARRATOR
I forgot. I was with someone. I met a girl.

NICK
A girl. That's worth all this? Priorities, Bro.

Nick slams out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Would I want Sionna to meet him? He set a kid's yard on fire once over a girlfriend. Everybody was afraid of Nick.

INT. DOCTOR'S OFFICE / HOSPITAL HALL - DAY

DR. CARSON faces Narrator.

(SOUND: distant siren, hospital PA)

DR. CARSON
Blood pressure's dangerously high. Risk of stroke. Emergency room. Now.

Nick arrives red-faced. They load Ma.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

Ma dozes. Narrator asleep in a chair. Jerks awake — nightmare still on him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I dreamed I was a soldier. Nick ran at me and put the longest knife into my chest. Betrayal. It took fifteen minutes to believe it was only a dream.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Blue weekly pill box on the table. Sionna helped fill it. Nick sneers from the brown chair, boots on the stool.

(SOUND: TV low, refrigerator)

NICK
Nurse Nancy. This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

NARRATOR
You haven't even met her. Give her a break.

NICK
Don't talk to me like that, you little bastard.

NARRATOR
I'm not a bastard— What the fuck got into you? Was Ma on crack when she was pregnant with you?

WHACK. Nick's open hand cracks Narrator's head. Ear rings. Nick storms out. Door slams.

(SOUND: slap, door boom, ear ringing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Me and Ma and Sionna would be fine without him.

INT. COMMUNITY COLLEGE CLASSROOM / JAVA HUT - DAY (MONTAGE BEATS)

Narrator in English class among mixed ages. Then walking with Sionna; kids on skateboards laugh at them; she shrugs it off.

(SOUND: chalk, campus murmur, skateboard wheels)

SIONNA
Only when we become masters of ourselves can we deal with the world.

They kiss. Slow.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
I could never love anyone like I love you.

NARRATOR
I could never love anyone the way I love you either.

INT. JOE'S BAR - POOL ROOM - NIGHT

Packed. Smoke. AC/DC on the speakers. Nick spots ex LINDSEY with a DARK-HAIRED MAN. Nick pushes in. Narrator steps between.

(SOUND: rock music, crowd hush, bottles)

NARRATOR
Leave it, Nick. You don't even know this guy.

NICK
Keep out of it. You're next.

Nick's fist draws back toward Narrator's face — JOE and a BOUNCER rush; chaos; Narrator runs into the night.

EXT. CITY STREETS - NIGHT

Narrator sprints until his chest burns — to Sionna's duplex. Into her arms.

(SOUND: footsteps pounding, ragged breath, night traffic)

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

On the green suede couch. Her fingers in his hair.

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one who really cares about you does that.

NARRATOR
I love him. He's my brother.

SIONNA
You're becoming a bravely compassionate being. Don't let him stop your purpose.

NARRATOR
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you. And honor it.

They sleep. Dawn.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Months later. Sionna serves pie. Nick finally meets her — stares like she's alien. Handshake reluctant.

(SOUND: forks on plates, awkward silence)

NICK
University lady. What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
English major. Philosophy minor. College professor, if I can.

NICK
Sometimes all that school doesn't pay off. Harder for a woman.

SIONNA
Bachelor's degrees earned by women rose from about thirty-five to fifty-five percent. Diversity helps us learn. You can't keep a good woman down.

She exits to the kitchen. Nick's smile is thin, snake-like.

NICK
(to Narrator, low)
Take care of Ma.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT (FRAME)

Narrator in the chair again. Rain harder.

(SOUND: rain, cat purr)

NARRATOR
Murphy's Law. Just when you think everything's going your way—

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Empty. Quiet. Blinking answering machine.

(SOUND: machine beep)

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olson, Milwaukee County Sheriff. We need to talk regarding your family.

NURSE (V.O.)
Frodo Hospital, with an update on your family members.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Old stone. OFFICER DUPUIS, graying, thin mustache, official.

(SOUND: station chatter, phones)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Serious accident. Werner and Lowe. Your brother Nicholas is in serious condition. Your mother, Olivia Olson, passed away at the scene. Toxicology pending. We think he may have been drunk.

Narrator shakes. Styrofoam water cup.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Through glass: Nick's wild hair, tubes, machines. Narrator looks once.

NARRATOR
No. I'm not up to seeing him.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Ma's empty chair. Afghan. Narrator breaks — raw, on the floor.

(SOUND: his sobs, apartment silence)

NARRATOR
Die you jackass. Die.

Sionna holds him.

INT. FUNERAL HOME / CEMETERY - DAY

Modest casket. Few mourners. Dirt hits the lid — muted thunk. Narrator alone as eldest present.

(SOUND: thunk of earth, wind in trees)

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Nick yellow-bruised, chest tube, leg huge under the sheet. Tears.

NICK
I guess I really fucked up. Three Jack and Cokes. Then church. Vehicular manslaughter. Mistake.

NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Nick flares — trapped, harmless in the bed. Narrator walks out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I no longer gave two shits about Nick.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Years of strain later compressed: Narrator works, studies, grieves. Sionna cooks for Ma's memory, then — present — alone in the duplex. Knock. Nick, thinner from injury but still large, pushes in.

(SOUND: knock, gray cat, kettle far off)

SIONNA
Nick? I never would have expected you.

NICK
You're the reason I'm probably going to jail. You messed with his head. School. Crazy ideas. He had one job — Ma.

SIONNA
Let's talk rationally—

He backs her to the wall. Hand on her throat. Switchblade flicks open. Blade at her collarbone.

(SOUND: knife snick, her choked breath)

NICK
Break it off. Cash in a week. Leak one word — I have friends. Parents too.

Front door SLAMS open. Narrator.

NARRATOR
Holy shit — Nick—!

Struggle. Blade bites. Red on her shirt. Nick elbows Narrator, wipes the knife, runs.

(SOUND: body impact, knife wet sound, door, fleeing steps)

Narrator on the floor with her — pressure on the wound — blood — 911.

NARRATOR
Stay with me. Stay.

INT. SEATTLE RESTAURANT - DAY (FRAME RESUMES)

Narrator at a window table, cinnamon roll untouched. Puget Sound gray. He doesn't eat.

(SOUND: restaurant murmur, ferry horn)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They said if I hadn't held pressure she would have died. Her parents thanked me with eyes that still blamed my blood. She survived the shoulder, the lung... not us.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - DAY

Months after. Sionna in a chair, blank ocean eyes. Foam pillow memory gone; the stare remains.

(SOUND: quiet room, street faint)

SIONNA
I can't feel me anymore. As long as there's any doubt, I can't go on with you. Break. Therapist. Mom and Dad's.

NARRATOR
Stay. Please.

She goes to the bedroom. He leaves for work early — free fall.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles. Citrus and lavender. Narrator on a mat among a dozen bodies.

(SOUND: soft breath, distant traffic)

INSTRUCTOR (O.S.)
Gratefulness. Things are neither good nor bad, but thinking makes it so.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I still felt the void. And the DNA I shared with a man in an orange jumpsuit.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Narrator wakes from a dream, smiling, grabs a notebook. Cat Bozo beside him.

(SOUND: pen on paper, rain soft)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A sage on a green road. A dirty pizza box. Stop judging. All things illusion. Forgiveness is the key inside myself.

He opens a laptop. Types. Books a flight to Milwaukee.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREETS / OLD BUILDINGS - DAY

Rental car. Old Kirk Street brick. Alley dumpsters. Former pizza parlor now a cosmetology school. Narrator looks, lets it go.

(SOUND: wind, light traffic)

INT. DELI - DAY

BOB, grayer, thicker, grease on a burger. Bear-hugs Narrator.

(SOUND: deli clatter)

BOB
You got out. Did something. I'm proud of you, man.

NARRATOR
I'm here to see Nick. Let the shit go.

EXT. SIONNA'S OLD DUPLEX - DAY

Grayer paint. Naked porch. Narrator in the car, doesn't knock.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I love her. I want her happy. Leaving her alone is the right thing.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL - VISITING ROOM - DAY

Plexiglass. Phones. NICK opposite — older, scrawny, gray at the temples, bags under sad eyes. Fifteen minutes.

(SOUND: locks, muffled voices, phone pickup)

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR
I'm here to say some things. Listen.

Nick nods.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
You're an asshole. You killed our mother drunk. You held me down so you could have the big life. You tried to kill the love of my life and drove her away.

Beat. Breath.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
And I forgive you. Because it sets me free. It ends your control. I still love you for being my brother. Get better. I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You said I couldn't. I did.

Tears on Nick's face, unfettered.

NICK
You're so right. I am a huge asshole. You're a big man. I'm a small one. President Olson. I'm proud of you. I love you.

GUARD (O.S.)
Time.

They hang up. Nick is led away. Narrator sits with the yellow receiver, light.

INT. AIRPORT GATE - DAY

Narrator in the boarding line, nuts and tea in hand. Calm. Almost floating.

(SOUND: airport PA, roll-aboards)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Time whizzes. Sionna dug me up and planted me. Somewhere she's happy. I send Nick love and know I will not see him again by choice.

Attendant scans the pass. Narrator looks at the name on the boarding pass. Soft, to himself—

NARRATOR
That's me. President Peter Olson.

He almost laughs. Boards.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Same chair. Rain. Cat. Bag unpacked, trip over. Narrator faces us one last time — quieter, free.

(SOUND: rain, ferry horn far away)

NARRATOR
This has been Nick and me.

He turns off the lamp.

FADE OUT.

THE END
